    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The single most important line on a fuel transfer pump is not the flow rate. It is the list of fuels the maker says it is built for. Plenty of cheap 12V pumps are diesel-only, and running gasoline through one is a fire risk, not a shortcut.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-a-pump" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Use a Fuel Transfer Pump at All
          </h2>

          <p>
            A full five-gallon can of gasoline weighs around 30 pounds. Tipping that into a generator's fill neck, a tractor tank or a boat's deck fill is awkward even in daylight, and the classic result is a splash of fuel over a warm engine or down the side of the machine. A portable fuel pump lets the can stay on the ground while a hose and nozzle do the work. Less lifting means less spilling, and less spilling means less fire risk and less fuel soaking into the ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second reason is volume. Anyone refueling equipment from a 55-gallon drum or a pickup-bed transfer tank cannot tip it at all. A drum or tank pump is the only sensible way to get diesel into a skid steer, kerosene into a heater tank or gasoline into a fleet of mowers. This is where 12V pumps and rotary hand pumps earn their place, and where flow rate starts to matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is the power outage. When the grid is down for days, a generator becomes the most fuel-hungry thing you own, and refueling it every several hours from heavy cans gets old fast. A battery operated transfer pump with an auto-stop nozzle turns that into a one-hand job. If you have not chosen the generator yet, start with our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generator for power outages</a>, then come back for the pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            What a fuel pump should never be is improvised. Water pumps, including cordless tool transfer pumps, are not designed for flammable vapors, and their makers say so. Mouth siphoning is dangerous; swallowing or inhaling gasoline can cause serious lung injury. Everything in this guide is a pump the maker sells specifically for fuel, and the first job is to check it is rated for the specific fuel you have.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three broad categories: 12V DC tank and drum pumps that run from a vehicle or equipment battery, battery-powered handheld pumps that run on disposable or rechargeable cells for cans, and hand pumps that need no power at all. Each suits a different job, and most households only need one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Five Pumps Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table shows the type, power source and the job each pump suits. Flow figures appear only where the maker's number is well established. The fuel column summarizes what the manufacturer markets the pump for; confirm the exact variant on its label before pumping gasoline.
          </p>

