          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best fuel transfer pump for a home generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners refueling from five-gallon cans, a battery powered transfer pump like the TERA PUMP TRFA01 is the best choice. It keeps the can on the ground, moves fuel at a controllable rate and avoids the lift-and-pour spills that cause fires. Always shut the generator off and let it cool before refueling outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are 12V fuel transfer pumps safe for gasoline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the manufacturer specifically approves them for gasoline. Pumps such as the Fill-Rite FR1210G are listed for gasoline, but many cheaper 12V pumps are diesel-only because their motors and seals are not designed for gasoline vapor. Check the exact model's label and manual, and follow its bonding and grounding instructions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a battery operated transfer pump for diesel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, most battery operated and 12V transfer pumps that handle gasoline also handle diesel, and many pumps are diesel-only. Diesel is less volatile, so a wider range of pumps is suitable. Use a separate pump or flush the hose if you switch between fuels, and fit a filter if you are refueling diesel engines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I avoid static sparks when pumping fuel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Place portable containers on the ground, never in a truck bed or on carpet. Keep the nozzle in contact with the container's opening, fill slowly and do not get back into a vehicle mid-fill. When pumping between metal drums or tanks, bond and ground them as the pump manual describes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I siphon gas from my car into a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many modern cars have anti-siphon or rollover valves that block a siphon hose, and forcing one in can damage the filler. Never siphon by mouth; swallowing or inhaling gasoline can cause serious lung injury. Keep stabilized fuel in approved cans for outages instead of relying on your car's tank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What ethanol blends can a fuel transfer pump handle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the pump. Most fuel-equipment makers list compatibility with common blends like E10 and often E15. Higher blends like E85 need a pump specifically rated for them. Check the exact model's specification sheet before pumping anything above E10, since seals and hoses can degrade in incompatible fuels.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can I store gasoline for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Untreated gasoline can go stale within a few months, which is a common reason generators fail to start in an outage. Add fuel stabilizer when you fill storage cans, rotate the fuel into a vehicle on a regular schedule, and consider ethanol-free gasoline where available. Check local fire codes on how much fuel you may store.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
